# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Cache strategy is the deploy-config half of the stale-chunk navigation fix:
# the SPA entry (index.html) must ALWAYS be revalidated so a new deploy's
# content-hashed chunk filenames are picked up immediately. Hashed build assets
# are immutable and safe to cache for a year. Without this, a returning visitor
# can run an index.html that references chunk hashes the server has already
# purged — clicking a nav item then loads a dead chunk and the view freezes
# until a manual hard refresh.

# Immutable, content-hashed build output (Vite emits new hashes per deploy).
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Static fonts shipped from public/fonts.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Always revalidate the SPA entry document so chunk references are never stale.
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Do NOT "fix" the two rules above by adding a catch-all `/*  Cache-Control:
# no-cache`. It looks like it closes a gap for deep links (/cases/…,
# /settings/…), but:
#   1. There is no gap. Cloudflare Pages already defaults static asset
#      responses to `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, so the SPA fallback
#      document served for a deep link is revalidated anyway.
#   2. Pages does NOT resolve overlapping rules by specificity. A request
#      matching several rules inherits ALL of their headers, and a header set
#      twice is JOINED WITH A COMMA — a catch-all would turn every asset into
#      `Cache-Control: no-cache, public, max-age=31536000, immutable` and
#      destroy hashed-asset caching sitewide.
# Removing an inherited header needs the `! Header-Name` syntax in the more
# specific rule; that is the only supported override.
#
# Cache headers cannot help the case where a tab is ALREADY open across a
# deploy — that document is never refetched. Entry-graph recovery for that
# lives in public/boot-guard.js; lazy-chunk recovery in src/lib/lazyWithRetry.ts.

# NOTE: production security headers (CSP, HSTS, X-Frame-Options, etc.) belong
# here too — the headers in vite.config.ts only apply to the dev/preview
# servers, not the static Cloudflare Pages deploy. Adding them is tracked as a
# separate (security-gated) change; see the audit report.
